   "summary": "A study published in Molecular Plant in 2022 by researchers from various institutions in China and the USA investigates the role of horizontal gene transfer (HGT) in the evolution of land plants. The study identifies two major episodes of HGT that significantly influenced the early evolution of streptophytes and the origin of land plants. Key findings include a high frequency of HGT in charophytes and bryophytes, with a decline in seed plants. Many acquired genes are retained and affect processes such as stress responses, ion and metabolite transport, growth and development, and specialized metabolism. The study emphasizes the importance of HGT alongside other genetic mechanisms in plant evolution and suggests that the decline in HGT in seed plants may be due to stabilized environments or improved adaptive mechanisms rather than barriers to HGT.",
   "key_excerpts": "Two major episodes of historical HGT events, consisting of 177 and 107 gene families, respectively, are responsible for a majority of genes acquired during the early stages of land plant evolution (Figure 1A)., Despite their continuous occurrence, historical HGT events did not occur uniformly in the evolution of land plants., A vast majority of the genes acquired in the two episodes have been retained in descendant groups, affecting numerous activities and processes of land plants., Many genes acquired early in land plant evolution are known to participate in a wide range of activities and processes in seed plants., Our data, together with those reported earlier, clearly show that relatively recent HGT has occurred in charophytes and all major groups of land plants."
